// engine/level/bg_anim.emp — BgAnim: driver-keyed BG tile-band animation
// (HCZ-pillar technique, modeled on S3K's AnimateTiles_HCZ2). Table-driven:
// up to BGANIM_MAX_BANDS independent animated strips per act.
//
// Each band is a periodic pattern held in a contiguous range of BG tile
// slots. The engine translates the pattern by 1 pixel per (1 << rate_shift)
// units of the band's driver value:
//   fine  (step & 7)  — selects one of 8 art banks pre-shifted 1px each
//   coarse (step >> 3) — whole-unit rotation done by splitting the
//                        bank DMA into two wrapped pieces (S3K's
//                        word_27AB8 trick), no extra art needed
//
// THIS PROC IS AXIS-AGNOSTIC AND ALWAYS WAS — read this before concluding
// otherwise from a field name (a whole item was priced on the belief that
// vertical motion needed a different DMA shape; it does not). Nothing below
// knows which way a band moves. `col_shift` is log2 of the ROTATION UNIT in
// bytes and `step_mask` is the pattern PERIOD in px minus 1; the axis lives
// entirely in how the eight banks were pre-shifted and in what order the
// band's slots are placed on the plane, neither of which reaches this file.
// Both axes satisfy the one condition the rotate needs — that
// units * unit_bytes == tile_count * 32, which is what keeps piece 1 positive:
//   horizontal  cols units x (rows * 32) B, slots column-major (c*rows + r)
//   vertical    rows units x (cols * 32) B, slots ROW-major    (r*cols + c)
// An increasing driver scrolls a horizontal band LEFT and a vertical band UP
// (bank k is phase 0 moved k px toward decreasing coordinate, and the coarse
// rotate carries the same sign). tools/inject_editor_bg.py's BAND_AXES block
// is the authority on the emitter side and derives both fields from `axis`.
// Composite rule: art layers inside the banks whose period ALONG THE AXIS
// divides 8px are invariant under the rotation and stay glued to the plane
// (or may slide at an integer px/step rate within their own wrap);
// non-periodic layers translate at the full step rate.
//
// BgAnim_Table is emitted by tools/inject_editor_bg.py into the act
// data block (runtime-read — act data assembles after engine code,
// so nothing here may conditionally assemble on its symbols).
//
// Table format:
//   dc.w band_count                      (0 = system disabled)
//   then per band, a 44-byte record:
//   $00 dc.w driver       0 = Camera_X, 1 = Camera_Y, 2 = Logic_Tick (low word)
//
//                         WHAT EACH ONE BUYS, because the choice is not free either way:
//                         Logic_Tick is LAG-IMMUNE, so a band's phase cannot diverge
//                         between a recorded run and its playback (BG anims freeze during
//                         lag frames, the intended behavior; audit 2026-08-02). A CAMERA
//                         driver gives that up in exchange for the thing a timer can never
//                         do — the band stops when the player stops, which is what makes
//                         it read as depth rather than as a looping animation.
//
//                         THE DIVERGENCE RISK IS NOT WHAT IT SOUNDS LIKE, and it was
//                         checked rather than assumed (2026-09-03): Camera_X/Camera_Y are
//                         themselves DERIVED from the player, which is derived from the
//                         replayed input, so a camera-driven band is exactly as
//                         reproducible as the camera is. What a camera driver actually
//                         costs is that a band's phase is now a FUNCTION OF POSITION —
//                         two runs that reach the same place by different routes show the
//                         same phase, where the timer would show different ones. Nothing
//                         in this tree gates on BG-anim phase in either form (no replay
//                         fixture, no state hash, no effects gate reads BgAnim_LastStep or
//                         the band's VRAM), so neither property is load-bearing today —
//                         but a future determinism gate wanting a phase that does NOT
//                         depend on route must use driver 2.
//   $02 dc.w rate_shift   step = driver_value >> rate_shift
//   $04 dc.w step_mask    pattern period ALONG THE AXIS in px, minus 1
//                         (cols*8 horizontal, rows*8 vertical)
//   $06 dc.w col_shift    log2 of the ROTATION UNIT in bytes
//                         (rows*32 horizontal, cols*32 vertical)
//   $08 dc.w tile_count
//   $0A dc.w vram_dest    VRAM byte address of the band's first slot
//   $0C dc.l bank0..bank7 pre-shifted art, 1px per bank

module engine.bg_anim in bg_anim

use engine.types.{VramAddr}

// MODULE-LOCAL ON PURPOSE — do not re-home this to engine.constants (tried, item
// 30/F, reverted). `bg_anim_port` lowers this file STANDALONE and links it against
// an EMPTY symbol table, so the module must stay self-contained: an engine.constants
// value used as an immediate becomes a link fixup (the harvest makes those names
// link equates, not comptime constants), and the standalone link then fails with
// `unresolved symbol BGANIM_MAX_BANDS`. A real single-authority fix needs a comptime path
// from ram.emp to a CODE module's consts, which does not exist today.
//
// engine/ram.emp is NOT a third authority — it NAMES the constant (`[u16; BGANIM_MAX_BANDS]`),
// so it is a CONSUMER and cannot drift. The three that CAN drift are this mirror,
// engine/system/constants.emp, and tools/inject_editor_bg.py's literal.
//
// GATED, so this is not sync-by-hand: tools/test_bg_emit.py::TestBgAnimBandCeiling reads all
// three and fails on any disagreement, on build.sh's pytest lane. A span `ensure` was
// DELIBERATELY not used — unlike RASTER_MAX_PATCH (where ram.emp must spell a bare literal,
// giving two independent sides), ram.emp CAN name this one, so a span ensure would compare
// constants.emp against an array sized BY constants.emp: self-measuring, passing forever.
const BGANIM_MAX_BANDS = 4

const BGANIM_BANKS     = 8          // pre-shifted art banks, 1px apart (fine phase)

// The 44-byte band record — the comptime layout twin of the table
// tools/inject_editor_bg.py emits (the OTHER author of this layout; see the
// LOCKSTEP note in the emitter). The runtime walk stays `(a3)+` streams —
// this struct exists so every hand-tracked cursor offset below is DERIVED
// from the layout instead of a magic number.
struct bganim_band {
    driver:     u16,                // 0 = Camera_X, 1 = Camera_Y, 2 = Logic_Tick (low word,
                                    // lag-immune — record/playback phase can't diverge)
    rate_shift: u16,                // step = driver_value >> rate_shift
    step_mask:  u16,                // pattern period along the axis in px, minus 1
                                    // (cols*8 horizontal, rows*8 vertical)
    col_shift:  u16,                // log2 of the ROTATION UNIT in bytes — the name is
                                    // historical (the horizontal arm shipped first);
                                    // rows*32 horizontal, cols*32 vertical
    tile_count: u16,
    vram_dest:  VramAddr,           // VRAM byte address of the band's first slot
    banks:      [*u8; BGANIM_BANKS], // bank0..bank7 pre-shifted art, 1px per bank
}
ensure(sizeof(bganim_band) == 44, "bganim_band drifted from the inject_editor_bg.py record layout: {sizeof(bganim_band)}")

// Walk-cursor displacements, derived from the record. After the step
// computation the cursor sits at col_shift (driver/rate_shift/step_mask
// consumed), so "to the banks" is one distance used twice: as the bank-read
// displacement and as the .skip_band advance over the unread fixed fields.
const BGANIM_COL_SHIFT_TO_BANKS = offsetof(bganim_band, banks) - offsetof(bganim_band, col_shift)   // = 6
const BGANIM_BANKS_BYTES        = sizeof(bganim_band) - offsetof(bganim_band, banks)                // = 32
const BGANIM_SPILL_BYTES        = 6 // the 3-word piece2-len/dest/piece1-len stack spill below
const TILE_BYTES_SHIFT          = 5 // log2(32 bytes per 8x8 tile)

// QueueDMA_Deferrable is an .emp-owned proc (engine.dma_queue) — its real
// signature carries the contract (`(d1, d2, d3) clobbers(d0-d4/a1-a2)
// out(carry: dropped)`); it resolves at link.

// -----------------------------------------------
// BgAnim_Init — force a bank DMA on each band's first Update.
// In: none. Clobbers: d0
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BgAnim_Init () clobbers(d0/a0) {
        // DEBUG only: seed the table selector with the act's own table, so a shape that
        // HAS a selector starts exactly where the plain shape permanently sits. See the
        // BgAnim_Table_Ptr block in engine/ram.emp for why the selector is a pointer.
        //
        // `lea` + store, NOT `move.l #BgAnim_Table, BgAnim_Table_Ptr`: sigil refuses a
        // link-time immediate combined with another symbolic operand ("[lower.imm-link]
        // ... not yet supported"), measured on this very line. That is why the DEBUG
        // shape's clobber set carries a0 — both callers in this tree already declare
        // d0-d7/a0-a6, so it costs nothing, and it is declared in BOTH shapes so the
        // published contract does not itself become shape-divergent.
        if DEBUG == 1 {
                lea     BgAnim_Table, a0
                move.l  a0, BgAnim_Table_Ptr    // (BgAnim_Table_Ptr).w
        }
        moveq   #-1, d0
        move.l  d0, BgAnim_LastStep             // (BgAnim_LastStep).w
        move.l  d0, BgAnim_LastStep + 4         // (BgAnim_LastStep+4).w
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// BgAnim_SetTable — DEBUG-shape band-table selector. Point BgAnim_Update at a different
// band table (the act's own, or one of the debug view twins tools/inject_editor_bg.py
// emits beside it), and force a full re-DMA so the new table's first Update repaints the
// slots rather than inheriting the outgoing table's committed step.
//
// WHY THE RE-DMA IS PART OF THE SWITCH AND NOT THE CALLER'S JOB: BgAnim_LastStep is
// per-BAND-INDEX, not per band identity. Two tables' band 0 occupy the same state word,
// so a switch between two tables whose bands happen to be on the same step would take
// the `.skip_band` arm forever and the new table would never paint. Poisoning the state
// here makes the switch atomic — there is no way to call this and get the stale picture.
//
// A table whose count is 0 turns the whole system off; that is what the act's own
// default table is today (tools/inject_editor_bg.py's `default_off` key), so "off" needs
// no separate mechanism.
//
// In:  a0 = band table (a `dc.w count` followed by count 44-byte records)
// Out: BgAnim_Table_Ptr = a0, every band's LastStep poisoned to the Init sentinel
// Clobbers: d0
// -----------------------------------------------
// DEBUG-shape only: the body — the `rts` INCLUDED — sits inside `if DEBUG == 1 {}`, so
// this proc emits ZERO bytes in the plain shape (the Debug_AssertObjLoop idiom,
// engine/objects/core.emp). The `rts` was outside on the first draft and cost the release
// shape TWO bytes, which moved every symbol after it and turned three committed gate cuts
// stale for a routine nothing in that shape can call. A proc whose only caller is
// DEBUG-gated must emit nothing, not almost nothing.
pub proc BgAnim_SetTable (a0: u32) clobbers(d0) {
        if DEBUG == 1 {
                move.l  a0, BgAnim_Table_Ptr    // (BgAnim_Table_Ptr).w
                moveq   #-1, d0
                move.l  d0, BgAnim_LastStep     // (BgAnim_LastStep).w
                move.l  d0, BgAnim_LastStep + 4 // (BgAnim_LastStep+4).w
                rts
        }
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// BgAnim_Update — per changed band, queues one or two deferrable DMAs
// (tile_count*32 bytes total).
//
// CALL CONTRACT, and read the next paragraph as a REQUIREMENT ON CALLERS
// rather than as a report of who calls it: this wants calling once per
// frame, after Parallax_Update. That is the design, NOT the wiring.
//
// WHO ACTUALLY CALLS IT TODAY, traced end to end 2026-08-24 — and the short
// answer is A PLAIN BOOT OF s4.bin DOES, every frame:
//   engine/system/boot.emp:349   move.l #Game.entry, (Game_State).w
//   games/sonic4/config/game.emp:59-60   ENTRY_ID = GS_OJZ_SCROLL_TEST,
//                                        entry = GameState_OJZScroll_Init
//   ojz_scroll_test.emp:549      installs GameState_OJZScroll_Update
//   engine/system/game_loop.emp:41  movea.l Game_State, a0  → dispatch
//   ojz_scroll_test.emp:821      jbsr BgAnim_Update — UNCONDITIONAL, right
//                                after Parallax_Update, then rts
// So the sole caller lives in a module named `test`, and that module is
// sonic4's BOOT ENTRY STATE. No special shape, no harness, no arranging:
// boot the ROM and this ticks.
//
// DO NOT RE-DERIVE "no shipping call site" FROM THE MODULE NAME. An earlier
// version of this very comment said exactly that. It was literally true —
// no *game-loop* call site exists — and it read as "the driver is dead",
// which is false and was one step from being handed to another lane as an
// engine gap explaining their runtime failure. The word `test` in the path
// is the whole trap; two lanes and one session memory hit it independently
// on the same day.
//
// The earlier wording here ("call once per frame from the main loop") was
// removed because it reads as proof of a call site to anyone who greps the
// proc and lands on it. That is the same perishable-claim-in-a-comment
// shape as effects_gen.py's slice-1 docstring, which propagated a wrong
// blocker into a cross-lane contract for four days (aeon da43a036). Stating
// intent and wiring as ONE sentence is what made both of them misread; if
// the game loop later gains the call, update the WHO paragraph — do not
// merge it back into the contract sentence.
// In: none. Clobbers: d0-d7, a1-a4
// (a3/a4 are movem-saved/restored by the body, but stay in the clobbers
// license: the preserves() round-trip proof is blocked by the mid-body
// 3-word SP juggle + the .queue_full unwind, so preservation is not a
// contract — callers must not rely on it.)
//
// PAIR-ATOMICITY CONTRACT (load-bearing call-position invariant): the two
// enqueues need no interrupt bracket because deferrable entries drain ONLY
// in VInt_Level, which cannot run until VSync_Wait sets VBlank_Ready —
// AFTER this proc returns (a lag-frame VInt drains nothing deferrable).
// Calling BgAnim_Update after VSync_Wait, or draining deferrable from
// VInt_Lag, would open a mid-pair drain window. Even with both pieces
// queued, the per-entry VBlank budget can land piece 1 and defer piece 2
// one frame — a one-frame coarse seam on the band (accepted; the pieces
// stay FIFO so piece 2 never lands first).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BgAnim_Update () clobbers(d0-d7/a1-a4) {
        movem.l a3-a4, -(sp)
        // The table this frame. The plain shape has exactly one and reaches it with the
        // same `lea` it always did — no cell, no load, no release cost. The DEBUG shape
        // reads the selector, so the lab can put a view twin in front of the walk
        // (engine/ram.emp, BgAnim_Table_Ptr; BgAnim_SetTable above).
        if DEBUG == 1 {
                movea.l BgAnim_Table_Ptr, a3    // (BgAnim_Table_Ptr).w
        }
        if DEBUG == 0 {
                lea     BgAnim_Table, a3        // (BgAnim_Table).l
        }
        lea     BgAnim_LastStep, a4             // (BgAnim_LastStep).w
        move.w  (a3)+, d7                       // band count
        assert.w d7, ls, #BGANIM_MAX_BANDS      // table wider than BgAnim_LastStep = a4 walk
                                                // off the state array + garbage DMAs. DEBUG-ONLY
                                                // backstop (asserts are zero bytes in plain) — the
                                                // emitter's <= BGANIM_MAX_BANDS assert is the
                                                // release defense
        beq     .exit
        subq.w  #1, d7
    .band_loop:
        // -- step = (driver value >> rate_shift) & step_mask --
        move.w  (a3)+, d0                       // driver select
        beq     .cam_x
        subq.w  #1, d0
        beq     .cam_y
        move.w  Logic_Tick+2, d0                // (Logic_Tick+2).w — low word of the
                                                // lag-immune tick counter
        jbra    .have_value
    .cam_x:
        move.w  Camera_X, d0                    // (Camera_X).w — integer px (16.16 high word)
        jbra    .have_value
    .cam_y:
        move.w  Camera_Y, d0                    // (Camera_Y).w
    .have_value:
        move.w  (a3)+, d1                       // rate_shift
        lsr.w   d1, d0
        and.w   (a3)+, d0                       // step_mask
        cmp.w   (a4), d0
        beq     .skip_band                      // unchanged this frame
        move.w  d0, d5                          // d5 = step (commit on success)

        // -- fine phase: bank pointer (cursor sits at col_shift; banks are
        //    BGANIM_COL_SHIFT_TO_BANKS ahead) --
        move.w  d0, d1
        and.w   #BGANIM_BANKS-1, d1
        add.w   d1, d1
        add.w   d1, d1                          // fine phase × 4 (pointer table)
        move.l  BGANIM_COL_SHIFT_TO_BANKS(a3,d1.w), d6  // d6 = bank base
        // -- coarse: whole-column rotation in bytes --
        lsr.w   #3, d0                          // step / BGANIM_BANKS → whole units
        move.w  (a3)+, d1                       // col_shift = log2(unit bytes)
        lsl.w   d1, d0                          // d0 = shift bytes
        move.w  (a3)+, d3                       // tile_count
        lsl.w   #TILE_BYTES_SHIFT, d3           // total bytes
        move.w  (a3)+, d2                       // vram dest
        sub.w   d0, d3                          // d3 = piece 1 length — positive only under the
                                                // emitter invariant units*unit_bytes ==
                                                // tile_count*32 (true on both axes); a drifted row
                                                // would send QueueDMA length <= 0 = a 128KB spray
        assert.w d3, gt, #0                     // DEBUG-ONLY backstop for that drift class
        move.w  d0, -(sp)                       // piece 2 length (= shift bytes)
        move.w  d2, -(sp)                       // dest base
        move.w  d3, -(sp)                       // piece 1 length

        // -- DMA 1: art columns coarse..end -> band base --
        moveq   #0, d1
        move.w  d0, d1
        add.l   d6, d1                          // src = bank + shift bytes
        jbsr    QueueDMA_Deferrable
        bcs     .queue_full                     // full — retry next frame

        // -- DMA 2: art columns 0..coarse-1 -> base + piece 1 (wrap) --
        move.w  (sp)+, d4                       // piece 1 length
        move.w  (sp)+, d2                       // dest base
        move.w  (sp)+, d3                       // piece 2 length
        beq     .commit                         // no rotation this step
        add.w   d4, d2
        move.l  d6, d1
        jbsr    QueueDMA_Deferrable
        bcs     .next_band                      // partial: piece 1 is already queued and lands
                                                // this frame (one-frame seam); the skipped commit
                                                // forces a redo ONLY when the step moves again —
                                                // a driver parked back on the committed step
                                                // leaves the mixed band until the next change
                                                // (ledgered: poison the LastStep word with the
                                                // Init sentinel here to force the redo)
    .commit:
        move.w  d5, (a4)
        jbra    .next_band
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
    .queue_full:
        addq.w  #BGANIM_SPILL_BYTES, sp
        jbra    .next_band
    .skip_band:
        addq.w  #BGANIM_COL_SHIFT_TO_BANKS, a3  // over col_shift + count + dest
    .next_band:
        lea     BGANIM_BANKS_BYTES(a3), a3      // past the 8 bank pointers
        addq.w  #2, a4
        dbf     d7, .band_loop
    .exit:
        movem.l (sp)+, a3-a4
        rts
}

// =====================================================================
// THE HYDROCITY WATERLINE'S ART HALF — EFFECTS-W1 item 9d
// =====================================================================
//
// The row remap has two halves and they are the same permutation applied to two different
// things. engine/level/parallax.emp's `.lp_remap` permutes plane-B SCROLL words; this
// permutes PIXEL ROWS out of a ROM image into a fixed VRAM run. S3K runs both every frame
// off one published quantity — `HCZ1_Deform` (sonic3k.asm:105849) and `loc_278C6` (:53981),
// both indexing one ladder by `Events_bg+$10`. Here that publication is
// `Waterline_Art_Row`, and it is the ladder ROW POINTER rather than the quantity, so this
// file carries no copy of the p derivation and cannot disagree with the pass about which
// row is selected.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT A BgAnim BAND, ruled by arithmetic and booked so nobody re-derives it:
// BgAnim precomputes EIGHT phase banks and rotates between them. The waterline has H+1 = 17
// states here (97 in S3K), CONTINUOUSLY selected by a camera quantity, and eight banks
// cannot express seventeen. Widening the bank set is not affordable either — at the d-9 ROM
// ceiling a new band caps at 15 tiles. A row gather costs 32*H bytes of ROM (512 here)
// against 8 * tiles * 32 for a strictly worse result. It lives in THIS file anyway because
// the DMA shape is BgAnim_Update's, which is the part worth sharing.
//
// THE NAMETABLE IS NEVER WRITTEN, in either half — design section 1.5 states this as a
// prohibition because the booking's own name ("row remap") reads as a nametable effect and
// the primary source writes zero nametable words. A plane cell pointing at these tiles sees
// the strip change under it; nothing here moves a cell.
//
// GEOMETRY. Source: 2 strips x 2H rows x 8 bytes-per-16px-row, flat and row-major.
// Destination: 2 strips x 2 tile columns x H rows x 4 bytes, COLUMN-major, because that is
// what a vertical run of 8x8 tiles is in VRAM. The gather is the transpose between them,
// which is why it carries two write cursors against one read cursor. The four tile runs land
// in VRAM in the order (strip0 col0, strip0 col1, strip1 col0, strip1 col1), each column a
// contiguous H/8 tiles — so a plane placing the ABOVE strip draws tiles
// VRAM_WATERLINE_STRIPS + 0..H/8-1 down its left cell column and + H/8..2H/8-1 down its
// right one.
use engine.level.scene_dsl.{CAP_ROW_REMAP}
use engine.level.parallax_dsl.{WATERLINE_H, WATERLINE_STRIPS, WATERLINE_ROW_BYTES,
                               WATERLINE_SRC_BYTES, WATERLINE_DST_BYTES, WATERLINE_TILES}
use engine.constants.{VRAM_WATERLINE_STRIPS}

// The three strides the loop needs, each DERIVED from the geometry above and each spelled
// once. A selective `use` of a constant is SILENT when the name does not resolve
// (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md section 2 — loud on functions, silent on constants), so the pins
// below are not decoration: a mis-spelled import lands here as 0 and the ensures catch it.
const WL_COL_BYTES       = WATERLINE_H * 4                      // one dest tile column: 64
const WL_SRC_STRIP_BYTES = 2 * WATERLINE_H * WATERLINE_ROW_BYTES // one source strip: 256
const WL_DST_STRIP_BYTES = WATERLINE_H * WATERLINE_ROW_BYTES     // one dest strip: 128
ensure(WATERLINE_H == 16 && WATERLINE_ROW_BYTES == 8 && WATERLINE_STRIPS == 2,
       "the waterline geometry imported from engine/level/parallax_dsl.emp is H = {WATERLINE_H}, row = {WATERLINE_ROW_BYTES} B, strips = {WATERLINE_STRIPS} — expected 16 / 8 / 2. A zero here is the SILENT failure mode of a selective constant import, not a geometry change")
// THE RESERVATION GUARD, and it reads the RAM layout rather than a mirror constant. The
// buffer's declared span is Waterline_Art_Buffer .. Waterline_Art_State_End (engine/ram.emp
// marks both), so this compares what was RESERVED against what the loop below WRITES. A
// short reservation is not a smaller effect — the gather writes the derived size regardless
// and the overrun lands in whatever engine/ram.emp placed after it.
ensure(extern("Waterline_Art_State_End") - extern("Waterline_Art_Buffer") == WATERLINE_DST_BYTES,
       "engine/ram.emp reserves {extern(\"Waterline_Art_State_End\") - extern(\"Waterline_Art_Buffer\")} bytes for Waterline_Art_Buffer but the geometry needs {WATERLINE_DST_BYTES}. WATERLINE_ART_BYTES in engine/ram.emp is the one number a change to H has to move on that side")
ensure(WL_SRC_STRIP_BYTES * WATERLINE_STRIPS == WATERLINE_SRC_BYTES,
       "the source strip stride ({WL_SRC_STRIP_BYTES}) times {WATERLINE_STRIPS} strips is not the emitted image size ({WATERLINE_SRC_BYTES}) — the gather would walk off the end of the game's art on the last strip")
// $B800 spelled as a literal, not as the imported VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, for the reason the pin
// block above gives: a guard that can pass by having its bound resolve to nothing is worse
// than no guard. engine/system/constants.emp holds the real one and carries its own ensure.
const VRAM_SAT_PIN = $B800
ensure(VRAM_WATERLINE_STRIPS + WATERLINE_DST_BYTES <= VRAM_SAT_PIN,
       "the waterline DMA ({WATERLINE_DST_BYTES} B at {VRAM_WATERLINE_STRIPS}) runs past {VRAM_SAT_PIN} and would overwrite the sprite attribute table")

// -----------------------------------------------
// Waterline_Art_Update — row-gather the two waterline strips and DMA them.
//
// CALL CONTRACT: once per frame, AFTER Parallax_Update (which is what publishes
// Waterline_Art_Row) and, like BgAnim_Update, BEFORE VSync_Wait — deferrable entries drain
// only in VInt_Level, which cannot run until VBlank_Ready is set, so the enqueue needs no
// interrupt bracket. There is only one enqueue here, so BgAnim_Update's pair-atomicity
// paragraph does not apply.
//
// In:  a0 = the GAME's waterline source image. It is an argument and not a well-known
//           symbol (BgAnim_Table's shape) so that a game without a waterline emits no art
//           at all: demo would otherwise have to supply 512 bytes it can never show.
// Out: none
// Clobbers: d0-d4, a0-a5 (d0-d4/a1-a2 of that is QueueDMA_Deferrable's own license)
//
// COST. The gather is 2 strips x H lines of {moveq, move.b, lsl, two move.l} — about 70
// cycles a line, ~2,240 cycles a frame at H = 16, and ONLY on frames where the published
// row changed. Standing still costs one long compare and a branch. That guard is S3K's
// (`cmp.w (a3),d1 / beq`, :53984) and it is why the art half can be ~9x the scroll half's
// per-line cost and still not matter.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Waterline_Art_Update (a0: *u8) clobbers(d0-d4/a0-a5) {
    if (Game.SCANLINE_CAPS & CAP_ROW_REMAP) != 0 {
    .cap_row_remap_art_begin:
        move.l  Waterline_Art_Row, d0           // (Waterline_Art_Row).w — &ladder[row][0]
        beq     .exit                           // 0 = no band remapped this frame
        cmp.l   Waterline_Art_LastRow, d0       // (Waterline_Art_LastRow).w
        beq     .exit                           // unchanged since last frame -> do nothing
        movea.l d0, a1                          // a1 = the ladder row (re-read per strip)
        movea.l d0, a5                          // a5 = the commit value; QueueDMA eats d0

        lea     Waterline_Art_Buffer, a2        // (Waterline_Art_Buffer).w — dest cursor
        moveq   #WATERLINE_STRIPS-1, d4
    .strip:
        movea.l a1, a3                          // ladder row cursor for THIS strip
        lea     WL_COL_BYTES(a2), a4            // a4 = tile column 1's write cursor
        move.w  #WATERLINE_H-1, d3
    .line:
        moveq   #0, d1                          //  4
        move.b  (a3)+, d1                       //  8   the ladder entry, 0 .. 2H-1
        lsl.w   #3, d1                          // 12   x 8 = the source row stride
        move.l  (a0,d1.w), (a2)+                // 24   px 0..7  -> tile column 0
        move.l  4(a0,d1.w), (a4)+               // 28   px 8..15 -> tile column 1
        dbf     d3, .line                       // 10   ~ 86 cycles per line
        // a2 has advanced exactly one tile column and a4 sits one past the strip, so a4 IS
        // the next strip's dest base. Not an optimisation — writing `lea` arithmetic here
        // instead would be a second expression of the same stride.
        movea.l a4, a2
        lea     WL_SRC_STRIP_BYTES(a0), a0
        dbf     d4, .strip

        // ONE DMA for both strips. The four tile columns are contiguous in the buffer and
        // contiguous in VRAM, so the split S3K makes (two runs, $2DC and $2F4) buys nothing
        // here — its two runs are not adjacent in HCZ's own tile map, ours are.
        lea     Waterline_Art_Buffer, a1
        move.l  a1, d1                          // src
        move.w  #VRAM_WATERLINE_STRIPS, d2      // dest (byte address)
        move.w  #WATERLINE_DST_BYTES, d3        // length
        jbsr    QueueDMA_Deferrable
        bcs     .exit                           // queue full: LastRow uncommitted, so the
                                                // whole gather is redone next frame. The
                                                // buffer rebuild is idempotent, which is why
                                                // this needs no unwind (BgAnim's .queue_full
                                                // exists to pop a stack spill this has none of)
        move.l  a5, Waterline_Art_LastRow       // commit only after the transfer is accepted
    .exit:
    .cap_row_remap_art_end:
    }
        rts
}
